The minimum vertex cover has **16** vertices. I found an independent set of 8 vertices and showed none of 9 exists. A cover is the complement of an independent set, so the minimum cover size is 24 − 8 = 16.

**Step 1 – An independent set of size 8.**
Take X013, X012, X002, X020, X003, X023, X009 and X016. Checking each pair against the edge list shows that no two of them are adjacent. Therefore the other 16 vertices form a vertex cover, so the minimum cover is at most 16.

**Step 2 – No independent set has more than 9 vertices.**
The 24 vertices split into 9 cliques. Every pair inside each clique is joined by a listed edge:
- A = {X014, X009, X018, X021}
- B = {X011, X016, X004}
- C = {X005, X015, X007}
- D = {X008, X006, X012}
- E = {X010, X022, X023}
- F = {X019, X020, X017}
- G = {X000, X001, X002}
- {X013}
- {X003}

An independent set can contain at most one vertex from each clique, so it has at most 9 vertices.

**Step 3 – No independent set has exactly 9 vertices.**
A set of size 9 would need exactly one vertex from every clique, so it must contain both X013 and X003.
- X013 excludes X019, X000, X021 and X007.
- X003 excludes X019, X014, X018, X008, X021 and X015.
- In clique A, X014, X018 and X021 are now excluded, so the set must use X009.
- X009 excludes X005, X015 and X007.
- That leaves no available vertex in clique C, which is a contradiction.

So the maximum independent set has 8 vertices, and the minimum vertex cover has 16.

