The minimum vertex cover has **16** vertices. This follows from König-free reasoning on independent sets: the complement of a vertex cover is an independent set, so the minimum cover size equals 24 minus the maximum independent set (MIS) size. The steps below show the MIS has exactly 8 vertices.

**Step 1 – An independent set of size 8 exists.**
Take {X003, X013, X019, X021, X007, X014, X004, X009}. Checking the edge list, no two of these are adjacent. So the MIS has at least 8 vertices.

**Step 2 – No independent set has 9 vertices.** A clique can contribute at most one vertex to an independent set. So if some vertices are split into k cliques, at most k of them can be chosen. The proof splits into three cases.

- **Case A: X001 is in the set.**
  - Its 12 neighbours are then excluded, leaving X000, X002, X003, X007, X008, X010, X012, X013, X020, X021, X022.
  - These split into the cliques {X000, X007, X010}, {X002, X013}, {X003, X020}, {X008, X021, X022} and {X012}.
  - At most 5 can be chosen, so the set has at most 6 vertices.

- **Case B: X001 is out and X012 is in.**
  - Removing X012's neighbours leaves 14 vertices.
  - They split into 7 cliques: {X005, X014, X016}, {X017, X023}, {X006, X022}, {X008, X019}, {X000, X011}, {X002, X003} and {X013}.
  - At most 7 can be chosen, so the set has at most 1 + 7 = 8 vertices.

- **Case C: X001 and X012 are both out.**
  - The remaining 22 vertices split into 8 cliques:
    - {X000, X007, X011}
    - {X002, X010, X013}
    - {X004, X018, X020}
    - {X005, X014, X016}
    - {X017, X021, X023}
    - {X008, X015, X019}
    - {X006, X009, X022}
    - {X003}
  - So the set has at most 8 vertices.

In every case the independent set has at most 8 vertices, so the MIS is exactly 8.

**Step 3 – Minimum vertex cover** = 24 − 8 = 16.

