The minimum vertex cover has 20 vertices. For any graph, the minimum vertex cover size equals the number of vertices minus the size of a maximum independent set (MIS), so I find the MIS: here it is 12, giving 32 − 12 = 20.

**Step 1 – Reduction.** X023 has exactly two neighbours, X021 and X006, and they are adjacent. So X023 can always be put in a maximum independent set. Removing X023, X021 and X006 leaves a 29-vertex graph G1, and MIS(G) = 1 + MIS(G1).

**Step 2 – Branch on X018 in G1.**

*Case A: X018 in the set.* Remove X018 and its 10 neighbours (X013, X005, X010, X022, X025, X014, X011, X026, X009, X000). In what remains, every choice below is forced or optimal:
- X017, X015, X012 and X028 are leaves, so take them. This removes X031, X019 and X030.
- X008 becomes a leaf, so take it and remove X024.
- X003 becomes a leaf, so take it and remove X002.
- X029 becomes isolated, so take it.
- X007 becomes a leaf, so take it and remove X016.
- The rest is the path X020–X027–X004–X001, whose MIS is 2 (X020 and X001).

That gives 1 + 10 = 11 in G1. The explicit set is {X018, X017, X015, X012, X028, X008, X003, X029, X007, X020, X001}. Every pair in it was checked against the edge list and none are adjacent.

*Case B: X018 not in the set.* The remaining 28 vertices can be split into 11 groups in which every pair of vertices is joined by an edge (cliques):
- {X030, X009, X028}
- {X017, X014, X000}
- {X031, X015}
- {X019, X012}
- {X007, X025, X016}
- {X001, X004}
- {X020, X024, X027}
- {X002, X029, X026}
- {X005, X022, X011}
- {X013, X003}
- {X010, X008}

An independent set can use at most one vertex from each clique, so this case gives at most 11.

**Step 3 – Conclusion.** Both cases give at most 11, and Case A achieves 11, so MIS(G1) = 11. Adding X023 gives MIS(G) = 12, so the minimum vertex cover is 32 − 12 = 20.

