The minimum vertex cover has 21 vertices. A minimum vertex cover is the complement of a maximum independent set, so the minimum cover size is 32 minus the size of the largest independent set. I show the largest independent set has exactly 11 vertices, giving 32 − 11 = 21.

**Lower bound: an independent set of size 11**

1. Take the low-degree vertices Tip_030, Tip_029, Tip_031, Tip_028, Leaf_027 and Leaf_026. None of these six are adjacent to each other.
2. This rules out their neighbours: Branch_021, Center_005, Bridge_009, Core_015, Leaf_024, Junction_017, Core_013, Center_006, Junction_018, Bridge_008, Hub_003, Hub_000, Junction_019, Core_014 and Bridge_010.
3. Of the remaining vertices, Center_007's only remaining neighbour is Hub_001. Add Center_007 and drop Hub_001.
4. Leaf_025's only remaining neighbour is Hub_002. Add Leaf_025 and drop Hub_002.
5. What remains is Center_004, Branch_022, Bridge_011, Core_012, Branch_023, Branch_020 and Junction_016. Branch_020, Branch_022 and Core_012 are pairwise non-adjacent, so add them.

This gives 6 + 2 + 3 = 11 independent vertices. So the minimum cover has at most 32 − 11 = 21 vertices.

**Upper bound: a partition into 11 cliques**

An independent set can contain at most one vertex from each clique. The 32 vertices split into these 11 cliques (every pair within each group is an edge):

1. {Tip_029, Bridge_009, Core_015}
2. {Tip_031, Leaf_024}
3. {Tip_028, Junction_017, Core_013}
4. {Leaf_027, Junction_018, Bridge_008}
5. {Leaf_026, Junction_019, Core_014}
6. {Tip_030, Branch_021}
7. {Hub_000, Center_007, Center_005, Hub_003}
8. {Bridge_011, Core_012, Center_006}
9. {Branch_022, Hub_001, Junction_016}
10. {Hub_002, Bridge_010, Leaf_025}
11. {Center_004, Branch_023, Branch_020}

The group sizes are 3+2+3+3+3+2+4+3+3+3+3 = 32, so every vertex is covered. Therefore no independent set has more than 11 vertices, and the minimum cover has at least 32 − 11 = 21 vertices.

Both bounds give 21.

